article_body=f'''<section class="article-header shell"><a class="underlined breadcrumb" href="/writeups/">All writeups</a>{label('FIELD NOTE / WEB SECURITY')}<h1>How I Got Paid for Turning an Image Fetcher into an Arbitrary File Read</h1><div class="article-byline"><span>By Raahil Muhammad Wildan</span><time datetime="2026-10-07">07 October 2026</time><span>Case-study summary</span></div></section><div class="shell article-layout"><aside class="article-aside"><div class="note-stamp">FIXED BEFORE<br>PUBLICATION</div><dl><dt>Class</dt><dd>SSRF / local file read</dd><dt>Surface</dt><dd>Image-to-Base64 API</dd><dt>Environment</dt><dd>Anonymized UAT</dd></dl><a class="underlined" href="{REPORT}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Original report on Medium</a></aside><article class="article-body"><p class="article-lead">An image-fetching feature accepted a URL and returned Base64. Allowing a local-file scheme turned that convenience into a server-side file read.</p><h2>The boundary</h2><p>The API accepted <code>file://</code> through its image parameter. Reading harmless system-identification files confirmed that the returned data came from the application server’s filesystem.</p><div class="request-note"><span class="mono">ILLUSTRATIVE REQUEST</span><pre><code>image=file:///etc/hostname</code></pre><span class="handwritten">The scheme changes the resource.</span></div><h2>The filter gap</h2><p>Equivalent paths written with dot segments, repeated slashes, or encoding passed a filter that rejected the obvious form. Validation and path normalization disagreed.</p><h2>Confirmed impact, stated carefully</h2><p>The confirmed result was arbitrary local file read. A configuration file produced a non-empty response; its contents were not decoded. Valid credentials, database access, and further compromise were not established.</p><h2>The lesson</h2><p>Restrict supported protocols, validate normalized destinations, repeat checks after redirects, and verify that fetched content is an image. Encoding a response does not protect its contents.</p><div class="article-disclosure"><span class="mono">DISCLOSURE NOTE</span><p>The organization is anonymized. The issue was fixed before publication.</p></div><div class="article-source"><span class="mono">FROM THE AUTHOR’S NOTEBOOK</span><p>This page is a concise companion to the original report.</p><a class="button button-red" href="{REPORT}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full report on Medium</a></div><div class="article-actions"><a class="underlined" href="/writeups/">Back to the notebook</a><button class="text-link" type="button" data-copy="{ORIGIN}{ARTICLE}">{icon('copy')}Copy article link</button></div></article></div>'''
page('Image fetcher to arbitrary file read','Writeups',article_body,ARTICLE,'A case-study summary by Raahil Muhammad Wildan: an image-to-Base64 endpoint, URL schemes, normalization, and confirmed local file read.')

